          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 9 — The Amazing World of Solutes, Solvents, and Solutions"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.888,
            0.029,
            0.922,
            0.044
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "143"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.084,
            0.41,
            0.103
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How to measure volume?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.106,
            0.741,
            0.147
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A tetra pack says it contains 200 mL buttermilk (chach) (Fig. 9.14). What does that mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.148,
            0.744,
            0.315
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You learnt in Curiosity, Grade 6 that "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "volume",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is the space occupied by an object. You also know that the SI unit of volume is cubic metres, written as m³. It is the volume of a cube whose each side is one metre in length. Volume of smaller objects is conveniently expressed in a decimetre cube (dm"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3",
              "styles": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ") or centimetre cube (cm"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3",
              "styles": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "). One centimetre cube is also written as one cc. Volume of liquids is expressed in litres (L) which is equivalent to 1 dm"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3",
              "styles": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". A commonly used submultiple of a litre is millilitre (mL) which is equivalent to 1 cm"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3",
              "styles": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.766,
            0.07,
            0.91,
            0.272
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.766,
                0.07,
                0.91,
                0.272
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_9_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.75,
                0.277,
                0.92,
                0.309
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 9.14: A pack of buttermilk of 200 mL"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.317,
            0.598,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "One of the common apparatuses used to measure the volume of liquids is a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "measuring cylinder",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". It is a narrow transparent cylindrical container with one side open and the other side closed as shown in Fig. 9.15. There are markings on the transparent body of the cylinder that indicate the volume of liquid in the measuring cylinder. We can use it to measure the desired amount of a liquid."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.484,
            0.6,
            0.577
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Measuring cylinders are available in different sizes to measure volume— 5 mL, 10 mL, 25 mL, 50 mL, 100 mL, 250 mL, etc (Fig. 9.15). How accurately can these measuring cylinders measure?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.611,
            0.316,
            0.962,
            0.554
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.611,
                0.316,
                0.962,
                0.554
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_9_image_10.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 12,
              "bbox": [
                0.626,
                0.559,
                0.959,
                0.591
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 9.15: Measuring cylinders of different capacities"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.579,
            0.27,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us find out!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.619,
            0.669,
            0.643
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 9.4: Let us observe and calculate"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.655,
            0.743,
            0.715
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In Curiosity, Grade 6, chapter ‘Temperature and Its Measurement’, you learnt how to use the thermometer and to find its smallest reading; you can do the same with a measuring cylinder."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.716,
            0.742,
            0.753
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Take a measuring cylinder and observe it carefully. Note down the following:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.755,
            0.615,
            0.774
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z What is the maximum volume it can measure?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.774,
            0.743,
            0.831
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now look at the measuring cylinder (Fig. 9.16) carefully. The cylinder is marked as 100 mL; therefore, it can measure volume up to 100 mL."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.832,
            0.743,
            0.871
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What is the smallest volume it can measure? Look at the measuring cylinder again."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.872,
            0.743,
            0.911
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How much is the volume difference indicated between the two bigger marks (for example, between 10 mL and 20 mL)?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
